1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:02,910
Throw a tennis ball at a brick wall,

2
00:00:02,770 --> 00:00:05,500
and you already know how the story ends.

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,610
It bounces back, every single time.

4
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:12,600
In the subatomic world, though, those rules break.

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,880
An electron or a radioactive particle can act

6
00:00:15,730 --> 00:00:18,460
like a wave and slip straight through a

7
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:22,240
solid energy barrier it has no business crossing

8
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:24,600
a phenomenon called quantum tunneling.

9
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:29,630
For decades, physicists assumed that trick belonged exclusively

10
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:32,770
to single atoms and invisible particles.

11
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:36,300
Then, the twenty twenty-five Nobel Prize in Physics

12
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:39,950
honored an experiment that shattered that boundary.

13
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,900
Using a superconducting electrical circuit on a chip

14
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:44,510
big enough to hold in the palm of

15
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:48,470
your hand, three physicists watched an entire circuit

16
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:52,560
sitting calmly at zero voltage suddenly tunnel straight

17
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,140
through a barrier and snap into a voltage

18
00:00:54,990 --> 00:00:58,540
state behaving, all at once, like a single

19
00:00:58,390 --> 00:00:59,277
giant atom.

20
00:00:59,977 --> 00:01:03,400
Wait why can't a tennis ball tunnel through

21
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:05,260
a wall in the first place?

22
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,370
Because a tennis ball isn't one quantum object.

23
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,240
It is trillions of trillions of atoms all

24
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:16,370
vibrating, colliding, and jiggling out of sync.

25
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,710
Even if one atom tries to act like

26
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:22,020
a wave, its neighbors instantly scramble it.

27
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:24,790
To see quantum behavior at human scale, you

28
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:28,090
somehow need billions of particles to stop acting

29
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:31,250
like a chaotic crowd and start moving like

30
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,590
a single synchronized dancer.

31
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:36,060
That is what happens inside a superconductor.

32
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,790
Cool a metal like niobium close to absolute

33
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:44,860
zero, and electrons stop bouncing off the crystal

34
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:45,770
lattice.

35
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:47,850
Instead, they bind together into Cooper pairs.

36
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:51,320
And here is the magic: unlike ordinary electrons

37
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:54,070
that refuse to share the same state, billions

38
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:56,830
of Cooper pairs drop into the exact same

39
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:00,550
quantum wave, marching in total lockstep across the

40
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:01,160
whole chip.

41
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:05,310
Now, back in nineteen sixty-two, Brian Josephson already

42
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:09,060
proved that individual Cooper pairs can tunnel across

43
00:02:08,910 --> 00:02:12,230
a razor-thin insulating gap between two superconductors.

44
00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:16,540
That was microscopic tunneling one pair at a

45
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:17,120
time.

46
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:21,740
In nineteen eighty, theorist Anthony Leggett asked something

47
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,080
far bolder: could the entire electrical state of

48
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:28,030
the whole circuit billions of pairs locked together

49
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:31,810
as one single variable tunnel straight through a

50
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:32,586
barrier all at once?

51
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,610
To test Leggett's challenge, you need a way

52
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:39,410
to trap that collective wave and watch it

53
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:40,950
escape.

54
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:44,980
The centerpiece is a Josephson junction: two superconducting

55
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,440
metal strips crossing over an insulating oxide sheet

56
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:52,660
only a few atoms thick, forming a tiny

57
00:02:52,510 --> 00:02:56,550
square about a tenth the width of a

58
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,150
human hair.

59
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:00,810
Inside that junction, the billions of synchronized Cooper

60
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:05,280
pairs share one single electrical property the phase

61
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:07,720
difference across the gap.

62
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,190
And here is the clearest way to picture

63
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:13,860
how that phase behaves: imagine a marble sitting

64
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:17,750
inside a bumpy washboard of hills and valleys.

65
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:20,890
When no current flows, the washboard lies flat,

66
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:23,630
and the marble sits peacefully at the bottom

67
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:24,160
of a valley.

68
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:28,550
Now slowly dial up a steady electric current

69
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,330
through the circuit.

70
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:34,160
As the current climbs up toward just under

71
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:38,145
ten microamps the entire washboard tilts.

72
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:41,080
Look closely at what happens to the valley:

73
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,740
the hill in front of the marble gets

74
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:46,790
shorter and thinner, but it is still a

75
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,110
real barrier.

76
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:50,270
As long as the marble stays trapped inside

77
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:53,190
that pocket, the phase cannot move, and the

78
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:56,700
voltage across the chip stays strictly zero.

79
00:03:56,833 --> 00:04:00,730
Current glides right through with zero voltage drop.

80
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,900
But the exact microsecond that marble escapes its

81
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:07,480
pocket, it starts tumbling rapidly down the tilted

82
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:11,350
hills and suddenly, a sharp measurable voltage snaps

83
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:12,247
onto the wires.

84
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,550
Sounds clean on paper so why didn't anyone

85
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,510
prove it immediately?

86
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:22,270
Actually, in nineteen eighty-one, researchers at IBM saw

87
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:26,180
tantalizing hints that a junction kept switching even

88
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:28,120
at ultra-low temperatures.

89
00:04:28,270 --> 00:04:30,860
But skeptics had a brutal objection.

90
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:33,880
Your chip might be sitting near absolute zero,

91
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,710
yet metal wires still have to run down

92
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,970
from your room-temperature laboratory to measure it.

93
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:44,520
Every warm wire radiates invisible room-temperature microwaves.

94
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:46,910
Those stray ripples can race straight down the

95
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:49,830
cable and violently kick the marble right over

96
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,890
the top of the hill perfectly mimicking quantum

97
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:56,570
tunneling when nothing quantum happened at all.

98
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:59,810
In nineteen eighty-four at UC Berkeley, professor John

99
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:03,500
Clarke, postdoc Michel Devoret, and PhD student John

100
00:05:03,655 --> 00:05:06,960
Martinis decided to eliminate every possible excuse.

101
00:05:07,450 --> 00:05:10,690
First, they built an ingenious shield right into

102
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:13,910
the cold wiring: tiny tubes packed solid with

103
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,300
microscopic insulated copper powder.

104
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:20,440
Smooth direct current glided right through, while stray

105
00:05:20,290 --> 00:05:24,440
high-frequency microwaves smashed into millions of conductive copper

106
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:26,670
grains and died out completely.

107
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:30,080
Second, before cooling into the quantum zone, they

108
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:33,660
measured every single electrical property of their junction

109
00:05:33,510 --> 00:05:36,220
right inside the warm classical regime.

110
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:39,110
That left zero adjustable fudge factors.

111
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:41,710
Once the chip got cold, quantum theory made

112
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:46,710
one rigid, unchangeable prediction either nature hit the

113
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,424
bullseye, or it didn't.

114
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:51,700
Then they fired up the dilution refrigerator and

115
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:55,340
watched the marble escape, thousands of times in

116
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:56,370
a row.

117
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:59,337
When the chip was relatively warm around a

118
00:05:59,278 --> 00:06:02,860
hundred millikelvin heat energy rattled the marble until

119
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:05,180
it hopped over the top of the hill.

120
00:06:05,110 --> 00:06:08,523
Cool the fridge down, and the thermal rattle

121
00:06:08,722 --> 00:06:11,870
shrinks, so escapes get dramatically rarer.

122
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,460
On a chart of the circuit's effective escape

123
00:06:14,310 --> 00:06:17,940
temperature against the fridge's actual temperature, the data

124
00:06:17,790 --> 00:06:21,430
marched cleanly down a straight diagonal line.

125
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,920
Until thirty millikelvin.

126
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:26,590
Right there, the curve broke away and went

127
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:27,580
completely flat.

128
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,460
Even as they chilled the refrigerator all the

129
00:06:30,310 --> 00:06:33,747
way down to nineteen millikelvin more than a

130
00:06:33,718 --> 00:06:36,930
hundred times colder than deep space the circuit

131
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:39,680
kept escaping as if stuck at thirty-seven millikelvin,

132
00:06:40,190 --> 00:06:45,020
landing squarely on the zero-parameter quantum tunneling prediction.

133
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:50,410
Still, a great experimentalist never stops there.

134
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:53,160
What if the thermometer was lying, or stray

135
00:06:53,010 --> 00:06:56,000
noise was still heating the chip to thirty-seven

136
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:56,390
millikelvin?

137
00:06:56,570 --> 00:07:00,010
In nineteen eighty-five, the Berkeley team pulled off

138
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:01,350
a checkmate control test.

139
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:04,200
They turned on a gentle magnetic field across

140
00:07:04,050 --> 00:07:04,610
the chip.

141
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:08,160
That magnetic field lowered the junction's critical current,

142
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:11,850
pushing the predicted quantum crossover below nineteen millikelvin.

143
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:16,430
Instantly, the flat plateau vanished and the escape

144
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:19,190
rate dropped straight back down to nineteen millikelvin,

145
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:20,980
matching the thermometer.

146
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:23,340
There was no hidden noise floor.

147
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:28,070
The entire macroscopic circuit was genuinely tunneling straight

148
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:28,974
through the barrier.

149
00:07:29,674 --> 00:07:33,870
Yet Clarke, Devoret, and Martinis went one giant

150
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:35,140
step further.

151
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:37,780
If billions of Cooper pairs trapped in that

152
00:07:37,630 --> 00:07:41,390
valley truly behave as one quantum particle, tunneling

153
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,730
isn't the only signature you should see.

154
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:47,650
Inside an atom, an electron cannot hold just

155
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:51,120
any arbitrary amount of energy it can only

156
00:07:50,970 --> 00:07:55,220
occupy discrete, quantized rungs on an energy ladder.

157
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,450
So in a companion nineteen eighty-five experiment, the

158
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:02,235
Berkeley team aimed gentle microwaves at two gigahertz

159
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:04,220
directly at the trapped circuit.

160
00:08:04,270 --> 00:08:07,150
If the circuit were classical, it would absorb

161
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,310
energy smoothly across a broad smudge.

162
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:14,310
Instead, as they slowly swept the bias current,

163
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:17,810
the tunnel escape rate stayed quiet… and then

164
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:21,520
erupted in three razor-sharp resonant peaks.

165
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:24,180
One peak where a microwave photon kicked the

166
00:08:24,030 --> 00:08:26,320
circuit from the ground state up to rung

167
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:26,510
one.

168
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:29,810
A second peak from rung one to rung

169
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:30,250
two.

170
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,910
And a third from rung two to rung

171
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:33,640
three.

172
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:35,700
Because higher rungs sit closer to the top

173
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:38,500
of the hill where the barrier is drastically

174
00:08:38,350 --> 00:08:41,510
thinner, every jump made the circuit tunnel out

175
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:45,200
dramatically faster letting the team actually see each

176
00:08:45,050 --> 00:08:46,830
discrete quantum level.

177
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:51,620
A centimeter-sized electrical circuit, etched onto silicon by

178
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:55,780
human hands, was absorbing light in discrete quantum

179
00:08:55,630 --> 00:08:58,640
packets just like a single hydrogen atom.

180
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:02,890
Humanity had built its first macroscopic artificial atom.

181
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:05,340
If watching a hand-sized chip act like a

182
00:09:05,190 --> 00:09:08,000
single giant atom just blew your mind, take

183
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:10,150
a second to hit that like button because

184
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:13,110
hidden inside those three peaks is the exact

185
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:16,399
reason modern quantum computers exist at all.

186
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:20,130
Look back closely at those three peaks from

187
00:09:20,340 --> 00:09:23,390
nineteen eighty-five, because hidden inside them is the

188
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:28,890
exact reason modern superconducting quantum computers exist at

189
00:09:28,740 --> 00:09:29,080
all.

190
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:31,100
Suppose you tried to build a quantum bit

191
00:09:30,950 --> 00:09:35,200
out of an ordinary superconducting coil and capacitor

192
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:37,190
a classic linear circuit.

193
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:39,900
Even if you cooled it near absolute zero,

194
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:42,840
its energy valley is a perfectly symmetric bowl,

195
00:09:43,210 --> 00:09:46,000
which means every single rung on its ladder

196
00:09:45,850 --> 00:09:48,380
is spaced by the exact same energy gap.

197
00:09:48,730 --> 00:09:51,790
Try sending a microwave pulse to flip your

198
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:54,550
bit cleanly from zero to one, and that

199
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,680
exact same pulse will immediately kick it from

200
00:09:57,530 --> 00:09:59,770
one to two, and two to three.

201
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:03,775
You can never isolate a clean two-level qubit.

202
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:07,050
A Josephson junction breaks that symmetry.

203
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:10,400
Because its energy valley curves unevenly, the spacing

204
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:12,610
between rungs shrinks as you climb.

205
00:10:12,770 --> 00:10:15,270
The jump from zero to one takes a

206
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:18,600
slightly different microwave frequency than the jump from

207
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:19,280
one to two.

208
00:10:19,430 --> 00:10:23,030
That tiny mismatch is pure gold: it lets

209
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:26,780
engineers tune a microwave pulse that talks exclusively

210
00:10:26,630 --> 00:10:29,510
to zero and one while ignoring every higher

211
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:30,570
state completely.

212
00:10:31,870 --> 00:10:35,770
Over the next decades, physicists turned that Berkeley

213
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:39,490
artificial atom into full quantum bits from early

214
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:43,170
Cooper-pair boxes in nineteen ninety-nine, to John Martinis's

215
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:47,700
phase qubits, to the transmon architecture Michel Devoret

216
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:50,430
helped pioneer at Yale in two thousand seven,

217
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:53,910
which powers today's leading superconducting quantum processors.

218
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:57,890
There is one last crucial twist that keeps

219
00:10:57,740 --> 00:11:00,000
this physics grounded in reality.

220
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,365
In their follow-up tests, Clarke, Devoret, and Martinis

221
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:08,325
also verified a haunting prediction by Amir Caldeira

222
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:11,980
and Anthony Leggett: connect even a tiny trace

223
00:11:11,830 --> 00:11:15,790
of electrical friction ordinary resistance to the junction,

224
00:11:15,790 --> 00:11:19,730
and the environment continuously eavesdrops on the phase

225
00:11:19,580 --> 00:11:20,100
state.

226
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:23,800
Like dragging that marble through thick honey, dissipation

227
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:27,800
chokes off macroscopic tunneling exponentially.

228
00:11:27,710 --> 00:11:30,780
That is why the quantum world feels invisible

229
00:11:30,630 --> 00:11:34,140
in everyday life, and why modern quantum computers

230
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:38,310
still hang inside massive golden dilution refrigerators cooled

231
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,950
below fifteen millikelvin, fighting every stray photon, magnetic

232
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:45,480
ripple, and microscopic chip defect.

233
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:49,600
The nineteen eighty-five Berkeley experiment didn't magically put

234
00:11:49,450 --> 00:11:52,800
a baseball in two places at once later

235
00:11:52,650 --> 00:11:55,840
experiments in two thousand pushed even further into

236
00:11:55,690 --> 00:11:58,690
macroscopic superpositions of opposing currents.

237
00:11:58,690 --> 00:12:02,400
What Clarke, Devoret, and Martinis proved and what

238
00:12:02,250 --> 00:12:05,090
earned the twenty twenty-five Nobel Prize in Physics

239
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:06,420
is something deeper.

240
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,670
Quantum mechanics does not expire at the edge

241
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:10,250
of an atom.

242
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:14,130
The wall separating the quantum world from objects

243
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:16,010
you can hold in your hand was never

244
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:17,430
a law of nature.

245
00:12:17,390 --> 00:12:18,540
It was just noise.

246
00:12:18,610 --> 00:12:22,150
If you enjoy deep, visual physics breakdowns like

